/E (the modern left is to erase all vestiges of influence from the time of European and Christian cultural dominance. This theory of the left, I believe, carries much more explanatory power than other theories. For example, some people say that the left is concerned primarily with liberating the oppressed. But if the left cared primarily about oppressor/oppressed relationships, then why do they not speak up about Chinese oppression of Uighurs, or Nigerian oppression of Christians? Categories of oppression only seem to matter when it is Western culture doing the oppression. So the unifying theme that best explains the modern left is not anti-oppression, but anti-colonialism.)
/Pg 16 0 R
/K [56]
>>
endobj
468 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H2
/P 218 0 R
/T (One can see why anti-colonialism is attractive)
/E (One can see why anti-colonialism is attractive)
/Pg 16 0 R
/K [57]
>>
endobj
469 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 218 0 R
/E (And one can see why anti-colonialism would be an attractive ideology. From a purely empirical perspective, it's hard to deny that the advantaged position of certain demographic groups derive at least in part from historical European Christian hegemony. It is also impossible to deny that Western cultural imperialism gave rise to many human rights abuses suffered by non-Westerners at the hands of Westerners. Introspection is always and everywhere a virtue, so for the Westerner, it makes good sense to introspect about past wrongs and to apologize for them. For the non-Westerner, such introspection may be encouraged as a means to either achieve reconciliation or to garner material concessions.)
/Pg 16 0 R
/K [58]
>>
endobj
470 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H2
/P 218 0 R
/T (What's missing is the relevant counterfactual)
/E (What's missing is the relevant counterfactual)
/Pg 16 0 R
/K [59]
>>
endobj
471 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 218 0 R
/E (But what's missing from the anti-colonial impulse is an assessment of the relevant counterfactual. Yes, Western colonialism led to abuses. But what other abuses, and how many and to what degree, would have happened had the Europeans not engaged in their colonial past? Can we fairly say that the world have been better had the Europeans not engaged in their colonial past? By what measure do we judge the result of Western imperialism versus the cultures that they displaced?)
/Pg 16 0 R
/K [60]
>>
endobj
