476 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 219 0 R
/E (Nigel Biggar tackles these questions in Colonialism: A Moral Reckoning. A moral philosopher, \(like Adam Smith\), Nigel Biggar is interested in telling the moral history of European colonialism, with a fair look at the entire spectrum of the humanity involved: from the European nobles who made policy from afar, to the lowly settler engaged in conflict with the natives, to the natives with varied attitudes towards European colonization, to the modern academics who look back at these stories through agenda-tinted lenses.)
/Pg 17 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
477 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H2
/P 219 0 R
/T (A much needed corrective to anti-colonial tropes)
/E (A much needed corrective to anti-colonial tropes)
/Pg 17 0 R
/K [5]
>>
endobj
478 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 219 0 R
/E (The book is a much needed corrective to modern anti-colonial tropes. Biggar does not shy away from acknowledging some of the horrors that resulted from European colonization, but he rightly points out that European colonization did much good around the world too. For example, he points out the British bore as much economist cost in trying to abolish slavery around the world as it profited from the slave trade prior to abolition. He also demonstrates, through primary sources, the wide extent to which many Europeans were motivated by genuine humanitarian sentiments.)
/Pg 17 0 R
/K [6]
>>
endobj
479 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 219 0 R
/E (Most importantly, he highlights the messy nature of human morality, showing us that it doesn't make sense to paint any culture with too broad of a brush, Western or otherwise. Within the European colonizers there were both saints and sinners, abolitionists and slavers, and the whole spectrum of humanity; just so amongst the native populations as well. Among the natives there were those who would have enslaved each other were it not for the European abolitionists, and there were others who welcomed European traditions as a relief against their oppressive native traditions. The picture that Biggar paints is simply one of messiness: when two cultures come into contact with each other, stuff happens; often, every kind of stuff you can imagine.)
/Pg 17 0 R
/K [7]
>>
endobj
480 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H2
/P 219 0 R
/T (Least sympathetic group: modern academics)
/E (Least sympathetic group: modern academics)
/Pg 17 0 R
/K [8]
>>
